  "conclusions": [
    "Composite native task accuracy is viable (Pets90.625%, Flowers85.9375%); both layouts remain usable.",
    "CLS-only native suffix inference is near binary chance at both tested layers. This does not support CLS-alone sufficiency as the explanation for current selector results. It does not measure all information decodable from CLS.",
    "At49 patches, each native-both-correct pair has a common correct bank mask in both datasets/layers. Four-way common feasibility is also complete except one Flowers image at block3.",
    "At16 patches, four-way conflicts on native-all-four-correct images occur in4/46 and6/46 Pets composites and2/38 Flowers composites at both layers. This is a small, concrete library-relative conflict signal, not a learned-selector advantage.",
    "Different-object margin gaps are larger, but direction-angle adjustment removes most of the16-patch contrast.49-patch adjusted coefficients remain descriptively positive, while classification feasibility is nearly saturated.",
    "No consistent depth-mediated reduction in conflict appears. A causal claim about shared information is not established by this screen."
  ],
  "design_obstacles": [
    "Task object and text-direction angle change together; overlap of their angle distributions is limited, especially Flowers. Matching during task construction would be preferable to relying on regression.",
    "At49 patches the panel creates a confidence tradeoff more clearly than an accuracy requirement for separate masks.",
    "Source images can recur and the panel is small. The four-way counts are secondary exploratory summaries, with no independent confirmation yet.",
    "Library choices use correct labels for feasibility diagnosis; common-mask existence does not show a shared selector can learn it, and missing masks from this finite library do not prove global impossibility."
  ]
}
